{"date": "2026-11-07", "post_id": 1509014, "title": "De Gerlache-Kocher Massif", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-7-de-gerlache-kocher-massif/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> De Gerlache-Kocher <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massif\">massif</a> looms on the visible horizon in this close-up of the edge of the Moon. The mountainous formation rises about 4 kilometers above its surroundings and is located very close to the lunar south pole. Boasting <a href=\"https://lroc.im-ldi.com/images/271\">permanently shadowed</a> crater floors that likely contain deposits of water ice and peaks that receive almost continuous sunlight for solar power generation, the south polar region is consider a prime location for the first human settlement on the Moon. <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2023LPICo2989.2037E/abstract\">In fact</a>, De Gerlache-Kocher massif itself has been consider as a potential <a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/nasa-narrows-artemis-landing-sites-to-9-key-regions\">landing site</a> for future crewed <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/mission/artemis-iv/\">Artemis missions to the Moon</a>. The remarkable telescopic image of the massif at the lunar limb was captured on October 31. Then, a favorable libration angle or <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/10836/\">lunar wobble</a> enhanced this oblique view <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-august-30-southern-moonscape/\">across the Moon's south pole.</a><br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>meteoric", "credit": "<a href=\"///Users/jtbonnel/Desktop/apodT/ap261107.html\">Alejandro Sanz</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"///Users/jtbonnel/Desktop/apodT/ap261107.html\">Alejandro Sanz</a>", "alt": "Mountains and craters at the lunar limb.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-7-de-gerlache-kocher-massif/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GerlacheKocherMassif_Sans.jpg?w=1500&h=1022&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 7 - De Gerlache-Kocher Massif</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-7-de-gerlache-kocher-massif/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: De Gerlache-Kocher massif looms on the visible horizon in this close-up of the edge of the Moon. The mountainous formation rises about 4 kilometers above its surroundings and is located very close to the lunar south pole. Boasting permanently shadowed crater floors that likely contain deposits of water ice and peaks that receive almost continuous sunlight for solar power generation, the south polar region is consider a prime location for the first human settlement on the Moon. In fact, De Gerlache-Kocher massif itself has been consider as a potential landing site for future crewed Artemis missions to the Moon. The remarkable telescopic image of the massif at the lunar limb was captured on October 31. Then, a favorable libration angle or lunar wobble enhanced this oblique view across the Moon&#039;s south pole.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: meteoric\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"de gerlache-kocher massif\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"De Gerlache-Kocher Massif\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-7-de-gerlache-kocher-massif/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GerlacheKocherMassif_Sans.jpg?w=1500&#038;h=1022&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GerlacheKocherMassif_Sans.jpg?w=1500&#038;h=1022&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Mountains and craters at the lunar limb.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-07T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-06T14:47:29-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"De Gerlache-Kocher Massif\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GerlacheKocherMassif_Sans.jpg?w=1500&#038;h=1022&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 7<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GerlacheKocherMassif_Sans.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GerlacheKocherMassif_Sans.jpg\" alt=\"Mountains and craters at the lunar limb.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> De Gerlache-Kocher Massif </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"///Users/jtbonnel/Desktop/apodT/ap261107.html\">Alejandro Sanz</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> De Gerlache-Kocher <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massif\">massif</a> looms on the visible horizon in this close-up of the edge of the Moon. The mountainous formation rises about 4 kilometers above its surroundings and is located very close to the lunar south pole. Boasting <a href=\"https://lroc.im-ldi.com/images/271\">permanently shadowed</a> crater floors that likely contain deposits of water ice and peaks that receive almost continuous sunlight for solar power generation, the south polar region is consider a prime location for the first human settlement on the Moon. <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2023LPICo2989.2037E/abstract\">In fact</a>, De Gerlache-Kocher massif itself has been consider as a potential <a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/nasa-narrows-artemis-landing-sites-to-9-key-regions\">landing site</a> for future crewed <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/mission/artemis-iv/\">Artemis missions to the Moon</a>. The remarkable telescopic image of the massif at the lunar limb was captured on October 31. Then, a favorable libration angle or <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/10836/\">lunar wobble</a> enhanced this oblique view <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-august-30-southern-moonscape/\">across the Moon's south pole.</a><br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>meteoric\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-6-a-farewell-to-swift/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261107/html"}